        <TopPick />

        <div id="why" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Why Polyurethane Shows Brush Marks
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every brush stroke leaves ridges. The bristles drag through the wet finish and leave tiny parallel furrows behind them. That happens with every brush, however expensive. What separates a smooth finish from a streaky one is whether those ridges have time to flow back together before the finish thickens enough to hold them. Brush marks, in other words, are a race between levelling and setting, and the whole technique is about tilting that race in the finish's favour.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three things decide who wins. The first is the finish itself. Oil-based polyurethane stays wet and mobile far longer than water-based, so it levels more forgivingly; water-based finishes skin over quickly, especially in warm, dry air, and lock in whatever texture the brush left. The second is film thickness. A coat that is too thin drags and shows every bristle; a coat that is too thick sags, runs and takes so long to cure that dust settles in it. The third is how much you work it. Every extra pass over finish that has started to set pulls it back into ridges, which is why both Minwax's Polycrylic directions and Varathane's water-based directions warn against over-brushing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Bubbles are a related but separate problem. They come from air that is introduced into the finish, by shaking the can, slapping a loaded brush on the rim, brushing too fast or rolling with the wrong cover, and like brush marks they are fixed in place if the coat sets before they rise and burst.
          </p>

          <p>
            The good news is that every one of these is controllable. The rest of this guide goes through the variables in the order you meet them: the brush, the surface and room, the can, the stroke, then what to do between coats and when something goes wrong.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="brush" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Kind of Paint Brush for Polyurethane
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most important rule is to match the bristle to the finish, and the manufacturers say so on the can. For water-based finishes, Minwax's Polycrylic directions call for a high-quality synthetic bristle brush, and Varathane lists synthetic bristle brushes, foam brushes and applicators for its interior water-based polyurethane. For oil-based finishes, Minwax's Fast-Drying Polyurethane directions specify a high-quality natural bristle or foam brush.
          </p>

          <p>
            The reason is the bristle itself. Natural bristle absorbs water, so in a water-based finish it swells, softens and loses its shape within minutes, depositing finish unevenly and leaving heavy marks. Synthetic filaments such as nylon, polyester and blends do not absorb water and hold their shape. Natural bristle comes into its own with oil-based finishes, where its split, fine tips hold plenty of finish and release it smoothly.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Finish</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best applicator</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Avoid</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Water-based poly, Polycrylic</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quality synthetic brush (nylon or nylon-polyester), foam brush on small flat work</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Natural bristle</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Oil-based poly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quality natural bristle brush, or foam brush</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cheap brushes that shed</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Wipe-on poly</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Lint-free cotton cloth</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Brushes, which defeat the point</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Floors, water-based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Flat pad or T-bar applicator, brush to cut in</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Foam rollers</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Beyond material, look for flagged or tipped bristles, where the ends are split into finer points, and a chisel-trimmed tip that comes to a clean edge. Those details let the brush lay finish down in a thin, even sheet rather than pushing a ridge in front of it. For most furniture a 2 to 3 inch brush is right: big enough to carry finish across a table top, small enough to control on rails and edges. An angled sash brush helps on frames and trim; a flat brush is better on wide panels.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you want specific recommendations, our guide to the <a href="/best-brush-for-water-based-polyurethane/">best brushes for water-based polyurethane</a> compares the synthetic brushes and pads worth buying, and our broader <a href="/best-paint-brushes/">paint brush guide</a> covers natural-bristle options. Whichever you choose, the best brush to apply polyurethane is a good one used only for clear finish. A brush that has been through a few paint jobs never fully releases its pigment and its bristles have lost their tip.
          </p>

          <p>
            Foam brushes deserve a mention because they are both recommended and blamed. They leave no bristle marks at all, which makes them excellent on small, flat, simple pieces. Their weakness is that they hold a lot of finish, release it unevenly at the start of a stroke and can trap air, so on large surfaces and with fast-drying water-based finishes a good synthetic brush usually gives a better result.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="prep" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Prep: The Surface, the Light and the Room
          </h2>

          <p>
            A smooth finish starts as a smooth surface. Sand bare wood through the grits to a finish of around 180 to 220, always with the grain, and do not go much finer, because a burnished surface gives the first coat less to grip. Water-based finishes raise the grain of bare wood, so many finishers deliberately wipe the sanded surface with a barely damp cloth, let it dry, and knock the raised fibres down with a light pass of fine paper before the first coat. That way the fuzz comes up before the finish goes on rather than inside it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dust is the enemy of a level coat. Vacuum the piece and the surrounding area, then wipe the surface: a tack cloth or a cloth dampened with mineral spirits for oil-based finish, a cloth barely dampened with water for water-based. Do this immediately before you start, and keep the room still while the finish is wet. A fan blowing across the work dries water-based finish faster at the surface, which is the opposite of what you want, and stirs up dust as well.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lighting is the step most people skip, and it is the one that lets you see marks while you can still fix them. Set a work light low and to one side so it rakes across the surface at a shallow angle. Under raking light every ridge, holiday and drip shows as a shadow or a change in sheen. Overhead lighting hides them until the finish is dry.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature and humidity matter more for water-based finishes. Varathane bases its dry and recoat times on 70 degrees Fahrenheit and 50 percent relative humidity, and warns that cooler temperatures and high humidity extend them. Warm, very dry air does the reverse, shortening the time the finish stays wet enough to level. In a hot, dry room, work in smaller sections. Cold slows everything down, which helps levelling but leaves the coat open to dust for longer and can leave it soft.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="can" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Stir, Never Shake, and Thin Only as the Label Allows
          </h2>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Stir the can. Never shake it.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Minwax's directions for its oil-based Fast-Drying Polyurethane say NEVER SHAKE in capitals, and its Polycrylic and Varathane's water-based directions say the same. Shaking whips air into the finish, and those bubbles go straight onto your project.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Polyurethane in satin and semi-gloss contains flattening agents that settle to the bottom of the can, so it does need mixing. Use a flat stir stick and lift from the bottom, folding the settled material up through the finish slowly. Minwax's directions ask you to stir before and during use, rotating product from the bottom of the can to the top. Water-based finishes often look milky in the can; Minwax notes that Polycrylic dries clear.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pour what you need into a separate clean container rather than working out of the can. That keeps the main supply free of dust and stray bristles, and gives you a container you can tap the brush against without the rim of the can filling up with half-dried finish that then flakes back in. Do not wipe the brush across the rim of the container; dragging bristles across an edge squeezes finish out as foam.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thinning is product-specific, so the label rules. Some water-based topcoats explicitly allow it: General Finishes' directions for its High Performance water-based topcoat say to thin as desired with distilled water, starting at 5 percent and going up to 10 percent by volume, and suggest its Extender to add open time and improve flow and levelling in dry climates. Other products give no thinning instructions at all, and some labels say not to thin. If yours is silent, do not improvise. With oil-based finishes, experienced finishers often thin with mineral spirits for better flow, but confirm the can allows it, because heavy thinning can affect drying.
          </p>

          <p>
            If a brushed finish keeps fighting you, consider a wipe-on polyurethane instead. Wipe-on formulas are thinner products made to be applied with a cloth, leave no brush marks by design, and build more slowly with more coats. They suit chairs, spindles and anything with complex profiles where a brush struggles.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Laying On and Tipping Off: The Stroke That Prevents Marks
          </h2>

          <p>
            For water-based finishes, many finishers dampen a synthetic brush with clean water and spin or blot out the excess before loading it, so the first stroke does not drag. Never do this with oil-based finish; if anything, condition a natural bristle brush with a little mineral spirits. Then dip the brush about a third of its bristle length into the finish and tap it gently against the inside of the container to stop drips. An overloaded brush puts a heavy band of finish down at the start of each stroke.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lay the finish on along the grain in a section you can comfortably keep wet, such as a single table leaf, a door panel or a few feet of a table top. Use moderate, even strokes and do not press; you are placing finish, not scrubbing it in. Minwax's Polycrylic directions describe applying a thin coat and brushing in one direction with the grain, and General Finishes advises avoiding pressure and excessive back-brushing. Work from the dry area back into the wet edge of the previous stroke, so the two blend rather than leaving a lap line.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then tip off. With the brush nearly empty, hold it at a low angle and draw the very tips of the bristles lightly along the full length of the section, end to end, in one continuous pass with the grain. Start each stroke just off one end of the board and lift out gradually as you leave the other end, like an aeroplane landing and taking off. This single light pass evens out the film, pulls any small bubbles to the surface to burst, and leaves the finest possible brush pattern for the finish to level.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then leave it. This is the hardest discipline. Once a section has been tipped off and has started to set, especially with water-based finish, any further strokes drag the skin and make marks worse. If you see a missed spot after a few minutes, leave it for the next coat rather than trying to patch it.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Order of work</h3>

          <p>
            Do edges and profiles first, then flat surfaces, so any finish that wraps over an edge is smoothed into the main surface. On vertical surfaces, use less finish per load and check for drips and runs a few minutes after each section, catching them with a nearly dry brush before they set. On panelled doors, work the panels, then the rails, then the stiles, following the direction of each piece of wood. Finish horizontal surfaces last, and where possible lay pieces flat so gravity helps the finish level rather than sag.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="bubbles" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. How to Apply Polyurethane Without Bubbles
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most bubbles are introduced before the finish ever reaches the wood. Shaking the can is the classic cause, and the labels prohibit it for that reason. Wiping a loaded brush across the rim of the can foams the finish on the brush. Brushing quickly and vigorously beats air into the film. Varathane goes further than most, telling users of its water-based polyurethane not to over-brush, shake or apply with a roller to avoid bubbles.
          </p>

          <p>
            Some bubbles come from the wood. Open-grained species like oak have deep pores full of air, and when a coat goes on, that air can rise through the wet film. It is worse when the wood is warming up, because the air in the pores expands, so finishing in a room where the temperature is stable or gently falling, rather than first thing as the heating comes on, tends to help. A thin first coat, worked well into the pores, seals them so later coats have less trapped air underneath.
          </p>

          <p>
            The tip-off pass is your main tool against the bubbles that do form. A light, slow pass with the bristle tips breaks most surface bubbles while the finish is still fluid enough to close over them. Watch the section under raking light for a minute or two afterwards. If small bubbles are still appearing, a second very light tip-off before the finish starts to set will usually clear them. Once the surface has skinned, leave any remaining bubbles for sanding between coats rather than chasing them.
          </p>

          <p>
            If bubbles appear every time despite all this, the cause is usually the applicator. Foam rollers and some foam brushes are notorious for pumping air into thin finishes, a problem our <a href="/best-roller-for-polyurethane/">guide to rollers for polyurethane</a> explains in detail. Switching to a quality synthetic brush or a flat pad applicator is often the fix.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="roller" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Rollers, Pads and Polycrylic: When a Brush Isn't the Only Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Can you apply Polycrylic with a roller? Minwax's own directions call for a high-quality synthetic bristle brush, and they do not list a roller. Varathane explicitly tells users of its water-based polyurethane not to apply it with a roller, because rolling introduces bubbles. On the other hand, General Finishes lists a roller among the acceptable application tools for its High Performance water-based topcoat. So the honest answer is that it depends on the product, and the label should decide.
          </p>

          <p>
            If your label allows it and you are coating large, flat panels such as cabinet sides, shelves or a big table top, a short-nap microfiber or very fine woven roller can lay finish down quickly and evenly. Roll slowly with light pressure, then immediately tip the section off with a brush along the grain to remove roller stipple and pop bubbles. Avoid open-cell foam rollers, which compress and release air on every turn.
          </p>

          <p>
            Floors are a different job. For water-based floor finish, flat pads and T-bar applicators lay a consistent film without the rotation that causes bubbles, and they let you work standing up. Our guide to the <a href="/best-water-based-polyurethane-for-floors/">best water-based polyurethane for floors</a> covers the finishes themselves, and stairs, which combine floor-grade wear with lots of edges and risers, are covered in our guide to the <a href="/best-polyurethane-for-stairs/">best polyurethane for stairs</a>. You still need a brush to cut in along walls and around balusters.
          </p>

          <p>
            Spraying is the other way to avoid brush marks entirely, and aerosol polyurethanes are an easy introduction on small pieces. The trade-off is overspray, masking and ventilation, and on anything larger than a side table most DIY finishers get better value from improving their brushing technique.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fixing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Sanding Between Coats and Fixing Brush Marks After
          </h2>

          <p>
            Light sanding between coats is what turns several imperfect coats into one smooth finish. It knocks down dust nibs, raised grain and brush ridges, and gives the next coat a surface to bond to. Follow the label for timing and grit. Minwax's Fast-Drying Polyurethane directions say to let each coat dry four to six hours, then sand lightly with 220 grit; the lower-VOC version of the same product gives three to four hours. Minwax's Polycrylic directions say to let it dry at least two hours and sand with very fine 220 grit, and not to use steel wool, which can leave particles that rust under a water-based finish.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Product</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Recoat after</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Between-coat sanding</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Coats suggested</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Minwax Fast-Drying Polyurethane (oil)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">4 to 6 hours</td>
