                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Minwax Polycrylic (water)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">At least 2 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">220 grit, no steel wool</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Varathane interior water-based</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sand if a coat has dried over 24 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Minimum 3</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">General Finishes High Performance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2+ hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Per label</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2 to 3</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Sanding is a light scuff, not a cut. Use a sanding sponge, a folded sheet or a block, go with the grain, and stop when the surface looks evenly dull with no shiny low spots left. Shiny patches are hollows the paper has not reached; dull, even sheen means the ridges are gone. Take particular care on edges and corners, where it is very easy to sand through the thin film to bare wood. Then remove every trace of dust before the next coat. Some products change the rules: Varathane's directions say sanding is needed if any coat has dried more than 24 hours, specifically to avoid brush marks, and Minwax's Ultra Fast-Drying Polyurethane for Floors says no sanding is needed if you recoat between two and 24 hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            To fix brush marks that have already cured, wait until the coat is fully dry rather than merely touch dry, then sand the area flat with 220 grit, or a finer grit if you are close to the final coat, until the ridges disappear and the sheen is uniformly dull. Clean off the dust and apply another thin coat using the tip-off technique. Do not try to fix marks by brushing more finish over a coat that is still tacky; that only drags the skin and makes them worse. For a final coat that is nearly perfect, finishers often sand very lightly with a fine grit and then rub the surface out with a fine abrasive pad and polish rather than risk another coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally, be patient about curing. Drying, recoat and full cure are different stages, and putting a piece into use too soon is how a perfect finish picks up marks of a different kind. Our guide to <a href="/how-long-does-polyurethane-take-to-dry/">how long polyurethane takes to dry</a> breaks those times down for water- and oil-based finishes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using a natural bristle brush in water-based poly</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Natural bristles absorb water, swell and go floppy, depositing finish unevenly and leaving heavy marks. Minwax's Polycrylic label calls for a high-quality synthetic brush. Save natural bristle for oil-based polyurethane, where its label recommends it.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shaking the can</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Shaking whips air into the finish and puts bubbles straight onto your project. Minwax's oil-based directions say never shake, in capitals. Stir slowly with a flat stick, lifting the settled flattening agent up from the bottom, before and during the job.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Going back over finish that has started to set</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Once a section has been tipped off and begins to skin, every extra stroke drags the surface and adds ridges. Water-based finishes set fast. Leave missed spots for the next coat and sand them flat between coats instead.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Laying it on too thick</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A heavy coat sags on vertical surfaces, runs at edges, traps bubbles and stays soft long enough to collect dust. Several thin coats, tipped off and lightly sanded between, level better and end up smoother than one or two thick ones.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sanding through at the edges</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Edges and corners carry the thinnest film and are the first place sandpaper cuts through to bare wood, leaving a pale spot that shows under every later coat. Use light pressure, a soft sanding sponge and fewer strokes on edges.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you apply polyurethane without brush marks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Use the right brush for the finish, stir rather than shake, and lay on a thin, even coat along the grain. Tip off each section once with a nearly dry brush held at a low angle, then leave it alone. Sand lightly with the grit the label specifies between coats, and keep the room still and well lit with raking light.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What kind of paint brush should I use for polyurethane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For water-based polyurethane and Polycrylic, a high-quality synthetic brush in nylon or a nylon-polyester blend, which is what Minwax's Polycrylic directions specify. For oil-based polyurethane, Minwax's directions call for a high-quality natural bristle or foam brush. A 2 to 3 inch brush with flagged, chisel-trimmed bristles suits most furniture.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best brush to apply polyurethane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The best brush is one with bristles matched to the finish, flagged tips and a clean chisel edge, used only for clear finishes. Synthetic for water-based, natural bristle for oil-based. Our guide to brushes for water-based polyurethane compares specific models, and foam brushes are a reasonable choice for small flat pieces.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you apply polyurethane without bubbles?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Stir instead of shaking, do not wipe the brush on the rim of the can, brush slowly with light pressure, and avoid foam rollers. Tip off each section with a light pass to burst surface bubbles. On open-grained wood like oak, a thin first coat helps seal the pores, and a stable room temperature reduces air rising from the wood.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you apply Polycrylic with a roller?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Minwax's directions specify a high-quality synthetic brush and do not list a roller, while Varathane warns against rolling its water-based polyurethane because of bubbles. Some other water-based topcoats do list rollers. If your label allows one, use a short-nap microfiber roller slowly on flat panels and tip off with a brush immediately afterwards.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you thin polyurethane to avoid brush marks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only if the label allows it. General Finishes, for example, permits thinning its High Performance topcoat with 5 to 10 percent distilled water and offers an extender for more open time. Other products give no thinning guidance or say not to thin. If yours is silent, use a wipe-on formula instead of improvising.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you fix brush marks in polyurethane?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Let the coat dry completely, then sand the marks flat with 220 grit or finer until the surface is evenly dull with no shiny low spots. Remove all dust and apply another thin coat, tipping it off carefully. Never try to brush out marks in finish that is already tacky; that makes them worse.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

