        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Applying polyurethane without brush marks is about giving the finish the chance to level: the right bristle for the finish, a can that has been stirred rather than shaken, thin coats laid on along the grain, one light tip-off pass and the discipline to leave it alone. Raking light shows you problems while you can still fix them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Between coats, sand lightly with the grit the label gives and remove every speck of dust. If marks do make it through, let the coat cure, sand it flat and recoat thinner. Several careful thin coats will always beat one heavy one.
          </p>
